I. Codex integration layer

OpenAI now supports Codex in the web app, CLI, IDE, and GitHub workflows. Codex can review PRs automatically, create PRs from its work, and the Codex CLI can read, edit, and run code locally. Codex also now supports plugins bundling skills, integrations, and MCP config for reusable workflows.

What your app should do with Codex
Generate task-ready prompts for Codex
Push task context into Codex
Store Codex session results
Attach generated diffs to tasks
Create PR drafts
Trigger Codex review on selected PRs
Trigger automatic reviews on every PR
Store review summaries
Track “accepted vs rejected” Codex suggestions
Re-run Codex on failed implementation attempts
Codex-specific features
One-click “Send to Codex”
Codex task packs:
implement feature
fix bug
refactor module
write tests
explain codebase
draft migration
Branch naming automation
PR title/body generation
AI handoff context bundle
Codex plugin support architecture
AGENTS.md / instruction-file sync
Review mode vs implementation mode
Best UX

Task page should have:

Context
Acceptance criteria
Relevant files
Constraints
Suggested command
“Open in Codex”
“Copy optimized prompt”
“Track completion”
J. Claude Code integration layer

Claude Code supports terminal, IDE, desktop, hooks, common workflows, and GitHub Actions. Anthropic also documents hooks for running commands or HTTP actions during Claude Code lifecycle, and their GitHub Actions flow supports @claude mentions in PRs and issues. Claude Code Desktop also supports CI-aware auto-fix and auto-merge patterns when configured.

What your app should do with Claude Code
Generate Claude-ready implementation prompts
Maintain CLAUDE.md project memory
Store reusable task recipes
Launch tasks with repo context
Sync architecture rules
Capture Claude outputs and summaries
Trigger repo hooks
Link GitHub issue / PR / task to Claude session
Claude-specific features
“Open in Claude Code”
Repo instruction sync
Hook event log
Prompt templates for:
understand codebase
implement spec
patch bug
update tests
review architecture
Session memory snapshots
Branch and commit suggestion generator
Auto-fix CI support mapping
Important design choice

Don’t treat Codex and Claude as interchangeable.

Treat them as:

Codex: repo-connected implementation + review + plugin workflows
Claude: deep iterative coding + local reasoning + hooks + GitHub issue/PR actions

That separation keeps the app sharp.

K. GitHub operating layer

GitHub PRs, reviews, checks, and check runs are the real delivery control plane. GitHub’s docs are very clear that PRs are the foundation for proposing and reviewing code changes, and that the Checks tab becomes powerful when you use checks rather than only commit statuses. GitHub Apps can also build CI checks around webhook events like check_run and check_suite.

Must-have GitHub features
Connect multiple repos
Connect orgs
Link project to repo(s)
Read branches
Read PRs
Read commits
Read issues
Read Actions / checks
Read review status
Read labels
Read releases
Actions in app
Create branch
Create issue
Create PR
Create draft PR
Update PR description
Request reviewers
Post comments
Summarize diff
Show failing checks
Retry checks
Convert task → issue
Convert spec → issue pack
Merge when rules pass
PR cockpit

