8. GitHub and AI workflow design, the right way

This part matters most for smooth coding.

Ideal loop
Idea becomes project
Project gets MVP spec
Spec becomes milestones/tasks
Task gets AI-ready context pack
Send to Codex or Claude
AI produces code / branch / PR / review
GitHub checks run
Failures return to task queue
Fixes applied
PR merged
Release notes generated
Project state updated automatically
You want the app to decide:
which tool is best for this task
what context to send
what files matter
whether human approval is needed
what happens if checks fail
what should be done next after merge

That’s orchestration, not task storage.

9. A practical “who does what” split between Codex and Claude
Use Codex for
PR reviews
repo-connected task execution
fast implementation bursts
GitHub-native workflows
plugin-based reusable workflows
standardized code review and fix suggestions
Use Claude Code for
deeper repo understanding
long iterative bug hunts
architecture reasoning
local coding loops
hooks-based custom automation
issue/PR workflows through GitHub Actions and local sessions
App should decide with routing rules

Example:

frontend UI task → either
large refactor → Claude preferred
PR review → Codex first
failing CI autofix → Claude or Codex based on repo/rule
release note generation → whichever template performs best
